{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>Of their flesh you shall not eat, and their carcasses you shall detest.</b> After having forbidden them for eating, He comes to explain 'and their carcasses you shall detest' — to say that it is forbidden to trade in them. This is 'and their carcasses you shall detest,' for thus did the Rabbis of blessed memory expound: one may not trade in unclean fish, as it is said, 'and their carcasses you shall detest.'" "<b>And behold,</b> there are eight things forbidden to trade in — four on account of their elevation and importance, and four on account of their lowliness. <b>The four on account of their elevation</b> is what the Rabbis of blessed memory said: one may not profit from consecrated property, because consecrated property belongs to the Holy One, blessed be He, and the Holy One, blessed be He, has no need for profit or expansion of wealth, for all is His, as it is said (Haggai 2): 'The silver is Mine and the gold is Mine, says YHWH of Hosts.' And the treasurer of the consecrated property is not permitted to trade in it or to profit from it; rather, it must be kept hidden away in his custody for the inspection of the Temple — and this concerns unconditional consecration. But conditional consecration, such as one who consecrates money on condition that they lend it to him and profit from it — this is permitted. And therefore the custom in all the communities is that the overseers of consecrated-property money lend it and profit from it, for on this condition did the owners consecrate it. But with unconditional consecration it is forbidden to touch it, and one may not profit from it at all; and concerning this they said: one may not profit from consecrated property — nor from charity funds, because the overseer of charity is forbidden to trade with charity coins or to remove them from his possession at all, for sometimes a captive may come who needs to be redeemed and there will be no ready cash in his hand, or he may need to feed the poor and it will not be in his hand and they would die of hunger, or he may need to clothe the naked — and this is not profit but a great loss. Also, one may not trade in a firstborn or tithe animal in which a blemish developed, which cannot be slaughtered in the Temple but must be eaten, as it is written (Deuteronomy 15): 'Within your gates you shall eat it' — but not that one should trade in them. And likewise fruits of the Sabbatical year are forbidden to trade in, for thus did the Rabbis of blessed memory expound: 'for eating' — and not for trading. <br><b>And the four on account of their lowliness</b> are these: the flesh of carrion and torn animals, swarming creatures and creeping things. If he has them in his possession, he is permitted to sell them, as Scripture says (Deuteronomy 14): 'or sell it to a gentile' — to say that Scripture permitted you to sell an animal that was kosher and your intention was to eat it, were it not for the occurrence of the accident that befell it. But Scripture does not permit you to trade in them as with other kosher and pure animals. And the swarming creatures are unclean fish, which is what is written here: 'and their carcasses you shall detest.' And therefore this was added so that the forbidden thing be equal whether dead or alive."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:24:49.446621745Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}